 since Michael's first  book was a critique of folklore I'm  gonna let our resident folklorist have  the last question thank you so much  Michael for this rich talk I have two  questions I like very much the idea as  you mentioned that there is no one  culture the same as there is no one past  and you mentioned that the neurologists  in Greece challenged this idea of the  past do you think that if Greek scholars  consider several pasts of Greece could  have a better concept of the Greek  identity so antiquity post antiquity  Byzantine past Ottoman past and current  situation so if we consider all those  pasts and I have a second question can I  ask how yes but may I answer your first  question while I still remember it and  then we'll go back to a second one but a  long day so I would not divide up the  past in that period izing way that you  just did you know in other words I don't  think that we could talk about the Greek  the classical past and the Byzantine  vast as separate entities there's a risk  there of drawing boundaries across time  in the same way that the colonizers drew  boundaries across space and so  conceptually I don't think it works very  well but I think you're right in another  sense which is that if we think the  different pasts as different types of  interpretation of the past and if Greeks  learn as I think they are learning and  we all have to learn this to accept the  idea that the richness of a country's  cultural heritage lies precisely in the  cacophony of different interpretations  that I think leads to some much more  interesting possibilities I don't think  that for example any to Byzantine ists  necessarily agree on what Byzantium  represented and probably represented  different things for them not only  me because their intellectual  trajectories are different but also  because their experiences for example of  the church and so on were different  so the past's the plurality of parts I  think shouldn't lie in a plurality of  periods there is continuity between the  ancient and modern world there's no  question about that I know farmer I just  to make that very clear but for example  that continuity is not necessarily about  Athens I did my master's thesis on a set  of folk song texts the so-called  Sheridan easement that demonstrate a  very close relationship with ancient  texts from the island of Rhodes in  Rhodes itself that that of course is  something that the nationalist folklores  didn't want to hear about because that  might have suggested the Grove should  become an independent state okay they  were in in the 19th century there was a  total confusion between culture and  state a nation-state is a piece of  territory with people on it culture is a  process and and this idea that we can  talk about cultures which I'm sorry to  say anthropologists were responsible for  propagating in the in the 19th century  now I think doesn't make any sense at  all and we see it because with the  increased permeation of the world by  people of very different backgrounds and  the fact that they are able to  communicate with each other we can see  that culture is in fact a very dynamic  process so I would say yes lots of pasts  because there are lots of  interpretations of what happened in the  past so your second question and your  notion of crypto colonialism is your  ambition to argue that this is worldwide  notion it it is applicable to all  countries so you started with Greece and  now you're comparing with Thailand do  you there and even even if it is not  even if countries are not colonized by  the Westerners do you think that your  notion would be applicable to worldwide  so my view of classification is what  mathematicians call a politic view in  other words I don't think that we should  try to make lists  different kinds of colonialism they have  real colonialism crypto colonialism  neocolonialism post colonialism it it's  not helpful  I simply throw the word crypto  colonialism into the mix because nobody  has talked very much about comparing  those countries that deny ever having  been colonized and have paid a heavy  price for that denial as well as for  what's done to them and it seems to me  that if after 20 30 50 years scholars  decide that okay this term was useful  but it's now outlived its usefulness if  it had some usefulness I would be very  happy I'm not wedded to the idea of  creating a new taxonomy I think that's  actually a mistake that scholars often  make and in fact you know the folk Lords  of the 19th centuries when all their  time developing absolutely untenable and  very complex forms of classification I  think it is useful to talk about crypto  colonialism just to tell people look the  evil that colonialism did and there were  some good things about it too but let's  talk about the evil crept far beyond  those red patches on the globe that  represent the countries that are  officially we'll call colonies the  British the French the Dutch the  Belgians and others besides and I don't  notice I don't say the Turks I'm not  even going to say the Ottoman Empire  because the Ottomans were had a very  different kind of quality and I think we  need a completely different  understanding of that dynamic first of  all the Ottoman Empire had a huge number  especially in the early years of Greek  bureaucrats Greek speaking bureaucrats  and military men the the the Admiral who  besieged Rhodes in 1480 was called  palaiologos Pasha I mean it's it's a  presumably a member of the Byzantine  royal family and the Greek speaker at  that so that's a whole different  ballgame but with regard to the Kotton  the category of crypto colonialism if  you just listen to the list that I again  that I suggested so Greece Thailand  Nepal Iran Iraq Afghanistan  I won I didn't mention he's very curious  is Iceland now I'm gonna finish by  telling you a story about Iceland that I  think will really make what I'm saying  much clearer one day I get an email from  an Icelandic colleague young  anthropologist inviting me to a  conference in Reykjavik where I'd never  been in my life and he said we wanted to  devote one day of the two-day conference  to the question is Iceland a crypto  colony well I propose was very flattered  and I also had discovered that there  were several scholars mostly very young  ones writing about the idea of Iceland  of the crypto colony I had always  thought that Iceland was a colony  straightforwardly of Denmark so I go to  this conference I remain skeptical for  about three-quarters of the day until  late in the afternoon one very  progressive Icelandic historian stands  up in protest against something someone  had said and in ringing terms in English  said but Iceland never was a colony and  the entire audience burst out laughing  because of course that's exactly what  you know Greeks say and most important a  fakir kinda knows and Thais have exactly  the same expression when can you tie in  any home that's exactly what they say to  say meaning and there he was saying the  same thing and I'd been saying that the  diagnostic feature of crypto colonialism  is that phrase the actual appearance of  the and the actual problems that it  creates for people living in those  countries are very similar to the  problems you find in post colonies so in  the end once we recognize the phenomenon  that it reached much further than it was  supposed to maybe we can just get rid of  the term and that's fine too I think we  have to be much more flexible than  scholars sometimes are in the way that  we adapt our theories to these very  complex and constantly shifting kinds of  boundary making processes of which  colonialism has certainly been one of  the most important thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

